WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.537
This practice turns bookstore business rules into constraints.

00:00:03.537 --> 00:00:09.692
Customer email must be present and unique, so think about the combination of NOT NULL and UNIQUE.

00:00:09.692 --> 00:00:13.467
Order-item quantity must be positive, which is a CHECK rule.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:16.807
Unit price cannot be negative, which is another CHECK.

00:00:16.807 --> 00:00:22.207
Every order item must reference an existing order and book, so foreign keys are required.

00:00:22.207 --> 00:00:24.712
Deletion behavior depends on meaning.

00:00:24.712 --> 00:00:31.872
Order items may cascade when their order is deleted, while deleting a book that appears in historical orders

00:00:31.872 --> 00:00:32.252
may need to be restricted.

00:00:32.252 --> 00:00:37.362
Try naming the constraint type for each rule before opening the sample patterns.

00:00:37.362 --> 00:00:41.542
The important skill is translating business language into database-enforced rules.

00:00:41.542 --> 00:00:47.287
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:47.287 --> 00:00:51.742
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:51.742 --> 00:00:54.657
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:54.657 --> 00:00:59.277
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:04.482
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:04.482 --> 00:01:10.847
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:13.137
not only whether the statement executes.
